 ],
 "digest": "This week's research engagement turns on financial device innovation and its political economy across the Global South. Correspondingly, we assess sovereign sukuk and development-bank financing as alternatives to IMF/World Bank conditionality; Asian bond-market regionalization and China-ASEAN connectivity as bottom-up capital-account integration; African payment-system evolution from correspondent schemes to digital platforms; and India's UPI infrastructure as foundational to economic participation. Concurrently, reporting from Casablanca, São Paulo, and Mumbai illustrates how financial centres navigate Northern governance pressures—currency defense against IMF orthodoxy, market discipline over fiscal space, and the contestation of monetary autonomy. We flag postcolonial theory on epistemic subordination and colonial echoes as meta-analytical frames, and note IMF lending's signalling effects and historical conditionality as structural constraints on policy autonomy in borrowing economies."
}